<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<usx version="3.0">
  <book code="1CO" style="id">1 CORINTHIANS</book>
  <chapter number="1" style="c" sid="1CO 1"/>
  <para style="s1">Apostolic Greeting</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 1:1"/>
    Paul, whom God called to serve as an apostle of Christ Jesus according to divine will rather than human appointment, together with Sosthenes our fellow believer in Christ,
    <verse eid="1CO 1:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 1:2"/>
    To the church of God’s people gathered in Corinth, to those who have been set apart as holy through union with Christ Jesus and called to live as God’s consecrated people, joining with all believers everywhere who call upon the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, who is both their Master and ours:
    <verse eid="1CO 1:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 1:3"/>
    May you experience the grace or unmerited favor and peace that come from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Thanksgiving to God</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 1:4"/>
    I continually express gratitude to my God for you because of the generous grace God has bestowed upon you through your relationship with Christ Jesus.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 1:5"/>
    This is evident because in every aspect of your life you have been enriched through your connection with Him, receiving both the ability to communicate spiritual truths effectively and the capacity to comprehend divine truth.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 1:6"/>
    This spiritual enrichment confirms that the message about Christ has been firmly established in your midst.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 1:7"/>
    As a result, you are not deficient in any spiritual gift or ability as you eagerly anticipate the moment when our Lord Jesus Christ will be fully revealed.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 1:8"/>
    He will continue to strengthen you until the very end, ensuring that you stand blameless when you appear before Him on that day when our Lord Jesus Christ returns.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 1:9"/>
    God is completely reliable and trustworthy, and it is He who has called you into intimate fellowship and partnership with His Son, Jesus Christ our Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Exhortation to Unity</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 1:10"/>
    Now I urge you earnestly, fellow believers, by the authority and character of our Lord Jesus Christ, that you maintain harmony in what you say and believe, avoiding all factions and divisions among yourselves. Instead, work toward being perfectly unified, sharing the same mindset and the same judgment.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 1:11"/>
    I raise this concern because members of Chloe’s household have informed me, my fellow believers, that contentious disputes have arisen among you.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 1:12"/>
    What I mean specifically is this: Each faction among you is claiming allegiance to different leaders, saying things like “I follow Paul’s teaching,” or “I align myself with Apollos,” or “I belong to Cephas’ group,” or even “I claim to follow Christ alone.”
    <verse eid="1CO 1:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 1:13"/>
    Has Christ been fragmented into competing parts? Was Paul the one who was crucified to accomplish your redemption? Or were you baptized into union with Paul’s name rather than Christ’s?
    <verse eid="1CO 1:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 1:14"/>
    I am grateful to God that I did not baptize any of you except Crispus and Gaius.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 1:15"/>
    This means no one can claim that you were baptized into allegiance with me personally.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 1:16"/>
    I do recall that I also baptized the household of Stephanas. Beyond these, I cannot remember whether I baptized anyone else.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 1:17"/>
    The reason this matters is that Christ did not commission me primarily to perform baptisms, but rather to proclaim the gospel message. Furthermore, I was to do this without relying on sophisticated rhetoric or philosophical cleverness, so that the message of Christ’s cross would not be stripped of its inherent power and effectiveness.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Word of the Cross</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 1:18"/>
    For the message about the cross appears to be utter foolishness to those who are on the path to destruction, but to those of us who are experiencing salvation, it represents nothing less than the mighty power of God at work.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 1:19"/>
    This truth is affirmed in Scripture where God declares: 
    <verse eid="1CO 1:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">“I will demolish the wisdom of those who consider themselves wise, </para>
  <para style="p">and I will disregard the intelligence of those who think themselves intelligent.”*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 1:20"/>
    So where are the wise philosophers now? Where are the respected scholars? Where are the skilled debaters of this present age? Has God not demonstrated that the wisdom this world prizes is actually foolishness?
    <verse eid="1CO 1:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 1:21"/>
    For since the world in all its human wisdom failed to recognize and know God through its own intellectual pursuits, God chose to use what seems like the foolishness of the preached message to bring salvation to those who believe.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 1:22"/>
    This approach makes sense because Jewish people typically demand miraculous signs as proof, while Greek culture seeks sophisticated philosophical wisdom.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 1:23"/>
    Yet we proclaim Christ in His crucifixion, which serves as an offensive stumbling block to Jewish expectations and appears as foolish nonsense to Gentile thinking.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 1:24"/>
    However, to those whom God has effectually called, both Jews and Greeks alike, Christ is revealed as the very power of God and the true wisdom of God.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 1:25"/>
    For even what might appear as God’s foolishness surpasses human wisdom, and what might seem like God’s weakness exceeds human strength.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 1:26"/>
    Consider the circumstances of your own calling, fellow believers. Not many of you were considered wise by worldly standards, not many held positions of power or influence, and not many came from families of high social standing.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 1:27"/>
    But God deliberately chose what the world considers foolish in order to shame those who think themselves wise, and God chose what the world views as weak in order to shame those who consider themselves strong.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="1CO 1:28"/>
    God also chose what the world regards as insignificant and despised, indeed things that worldly people consider nonexistent, in order to nullify and set aside the things that the world considers important.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="1CO 1:29"/>
    God did this so that no human being would have grounds for boasting in His presence.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="1CO 1:30"/>
    And it is because of God’s gracious work that you exist in union with Christ Jesus, who has become for us the embodiment of wisdom that comes from God. He is our righteousness, making us right with God; He is our sanctification, setting us apart as holy; and He is our redemption, purchasing our freedom.
    <verse eid="1CO 1:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="1CO 1:31"/>
    Therefore, as Scripture declares: “Let the person who boasts find his grounds for boasting only in the Lord.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 1:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">19 Quoted from Is. 29:14</para>
  <para style="p">31 Quoted from Jer. 9:24</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 1"/>
  <chapter number="2" style="c" sid="1CO 2"/>
  <para style="s1">Paul’s Message and Preaching</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 2:1"/>
    And when I came to you, fellow believers, I deliberately chose not to arrive with impressive oratory skills or philosophical sophistication as I proclaimed to you the testimony about God’s redemptive plan.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 2:2"/>
    For I had determined in advance that while among you I would focus my attention entirely on Jesus Christ, specifically on the reality of His crucifixion, and nothing else.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 2:3"/>
    Moreover, I came to you in a state of physical weakness, emotional fear, and considerable apprehension.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 2:4"/>
    My speaking and my preaching did not rely upon persuasive arguments drawn from human wisdom, but instead demonstrated the Spirit’s presence and power working through me.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 2:5"/>
    I took this approach so that your faith would rest firmly on the power of God rather than on human wisdom and intellectual argumentation.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Wisdom of God</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 2:6"/>
    Yet when we speak to believers who are spiritually mature, we do communicate wisdom, though it is a wisdom that does not originate from this present age, nor from the rulers of this age who are being brought to nothing.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 2:7"/>
    Rather, we proclaim God’s wisdom that has been hidden as a mystery, the wisdom that God predetermined before the ages began, intending it for our ultimate glory.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 2:8"/>
    None of the rulers of this present age understood this wisdom. If they had comprehended it, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 2:9"/>
    But just as Scripture says: 
    <verse eid="1CO 2:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">“Things which no human eye has ever seen </para>
  <para style="p">and no human ear has ever heard, </para>
  <para style="p">and which have never even entered into human imagination, </para>
  <para style="p">all these things God has prepared for those who love Him.”*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 2:10"/>
    For God has unveiled these truths to us through the working of the Holy Spirit. The Spirit searches and examines all things, even the profound depths of God’s own nature.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 2:11"/>
    For who among human beings truly comprehends the inner thoughts and nature of another person except that person’s own spirit dwelling within? In the same way, no one can truly know the thoughts and nature of God except the Spirit of God Himself.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 2:12"/>
    Now we have not received the spirit that characterizes this fallen world system, but rather we have received the Spirit who comes from God, enabling us to understand and appreciate the things that God has graciously given to us.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 2:13"/>
    These are the very things we speak about, not using words taught by human wisdom and philosophy, but using words taught by the Spirit, as we explain spiritual realities through Spirit-given language.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 2:14"/>
    But the person who remains in their natural, unregenerate state does not welcome or accept the things that belong to the Spirit of God, because such things appear as foolishness to them. They lack the capacity to understand these truths because spiritual realities require spiritual discernment.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 2:15"/>
    By contrast, the person who is spiritual assesses all things correctly, yet that spiritual person is not subject to accurate judgment or evaluation by unspiritual or natural people.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 2:16"/>
    For Scripture asks, “Who has come to know the mind of the Lord? Who could presume to instruct Him?”* Yet we who believe possess the mind of Christ and share His perspective.
    <verse eid="1CO 2:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">9 Quoted from Is. 64:4</para>
  <para style="p">16 Quoted from Is. 40:13</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 2"/>
  <chapter number="3" style="c" sid="1CO 3"/>
  <para style="s1">Fleshly Divisiveness</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 3:1"/>
    And I, fellow believers, found myself unable to address you as spiritually mature people, but instead had to speak to you as people still dominated by fleshly desires, as spiritual infants in Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 3:2"/>
    I fed you with milk rather than solid food, because you were not yet capable of digesting mature teaching. In fact, you still are not ready even now.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 3:3"/>
    This is evident because you continue to be controlled by fleshly impulses. For where jealousy and quarreling exist among you, does this not prove that you are still fleshly, and that you are conducting yourselves according to merely human standards?
    <verse eid="1CO 3:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 3:4"/>
    For when one person claims, “I belong to Paul,” while another says, “I belong to Apollos,” are you not demonstrating that you are operating on a merely human level?
    <verse eid="1CO 3:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 3:5"/>
    After all, what is Apollos? And what is Paul? We are merely servants through whose ministry you came to faith, and each of us served in the capacity that the Lord assigned.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 3:6"/>
    I planted the initial seed, Apollos provided water and nourishment, but it was God who caused the growth to occur.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 3:7"/>
    Therefore, neither the one who plants nor the one who waters has any intrinsic significance; what matters is God who causes the growth.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 3:8"/>
    Now the one who plants and the one who waters share a common purpose, yet each will receive his own individual reward corresponding to his own labor and effort.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 3:9"/>
    For we are co-laborers working together in God’s service; you are God’s field under cultivation, you are God’s building under construction.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 3:10"/>
    According to the grace of God that was given to me, I laid a foundation like a skilled master builder, and now another person is constructing upon it. But each builder must exercise care regarding how they build upon that foundation.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 3:11"/>
    For no one has the ability to lay any foundation other than the one already laid, which is Jesus Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 3:12"/>
    Now if anyone constructs upon that foundation using gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay, or straw,
    <verse eid="1CO 3:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 3:13"/>
    each person’s work will become evident and exposed. For the day of judgment will reveal it, because it will be disclosed by fire, and the fire itself will test and prove what quality of work each person has produced.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 3:14"/>
    If the work that anyone has built upon the foundation survives the test, that person will receive a reward.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 3:15"/>
    If anyone’s work is burned up and destroyed, that person will suffer loss, though the person themselves will be saved, yet only as one barely escaping through flames.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 3:16"/>
    Do you not understand that you collectively are God’s temple and that the Spirit of God lives within you?
    <verse eid="1CO 3:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 3:17"/>
    If anyone destroys God’s temple through divisiveness and false teaching, God will destroy that person, for God’s temple is sacred and holy, and you are that temple.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 3:18"/>
    Let no one deceive themselves with false confidence. If anyone among you considers themselves wise according to the standards of this present age, let that person become foolish in the world’s eyes so that they may become truly wise in God’s sight.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 3:19"/>
    For the wisdom that this world values is foolishness in God’s estimation. Scripture confirms this: “He is the One who traps the wise in the schemes of their own cunning.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 3:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 3:20"/>
    And again Scripture states, “The Lord knows the reasonings of the wise, that they are futile and empty.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 3:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 3:21"/>
    Therefore, let no one boast about human leaders or take pride in allegiance to particular people. For all things ultimately belong to you:
    <verse eid="1CO 3:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 3:22"/>
    whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas; whether the world or life itself or death; whether things in the present or things yet to come—all of these are yours.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 3:23"/>
    And you belong to Christ, and Christ belongs to God.
    <verse eid="1CO 3:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">19 Quoted from Job 5:13</para>
  <para style="p">20 Quoted from Ps. 94:11</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 3"/>
  <chapter number="4" style="c" sid="1CO 4"/>
  <para style="s1">The Apostles and Their Calling</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 4:1"/>
    Let a person regard us in the proper light: as servants of Christ and stewards entrusted with managing the mysteries of God.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 4:2"/>
    Furthermore, in this role as stewards, it is essential that a person be found faithful and trustworthy.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 4:3"/>
    But as far as I am concerned, it matters very little to me whether I am examined and evaluated by you or by any human court. Indeed, I do not even evaluate and judge myself.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 4:4"/>
    For although I am not conscious of anything I have done wrong, this does not mean I am thereby vindicated. The One who examines and judges me is the Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 4:5"/>
    Therefore, do not pronounce judgment on anything prematurely, before the appointed time, until the Lord comes. He will bring to light the hidden things concealed in darkness and will expose the motivations and intentions of human hearts. At that time, each person will receive whatever commendation is due from God.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 4:6"/>
    Now, fellow believers, I have applied these principles figuratively to myself and Apollos for your benefit, so that through our example you may learn the principle of not going beyond what Scripture teaches, so that none of you will become arrogant and prideful, siding with one leader against another.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 4:7"/>
    For who gives you any basis for thinking you are superior to another believer? And what do you possess that you did not receive as a gift? And if you indeed received it as a gift, why do you boast as though you did not receive it but earned it yourself?
    <verse eid="1CO 4:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 4:8"/>
    Already you act as though you are completely satisfied. Already you behave as though you have become wealthy. Without our guidance you have begun to reign as kings. And I sincerely wish you truly were reigning, so that we apostles might also reign together with you!
    <verse eid="1CO 4:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 4:9"/>
    For it seems to me that God has put us apostles on display as last in line, like prisoners condemned to death in the arena, because we have become a spectacle for the whole world to observe, both angels and human beings.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 4:10"/>
    We are regarded as fools for the sake of Christ, but you consider yourselves wise in Christ. We are weak, but you see yourselves as strong. You are held in honor, but we are treated with contempt and dishonor!
    <verse eid="1CO 4:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 4:11"/>
    Up to this very moment, we experience both hunger and thirst, we wear inadequate clothing, we are beaten and mistreated, and we have no permanent home.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 4:12"/>
    We toil and labor, working with our own hands to support ourselves. When people insult and revile us, we respond with blessing. When we are persecuted, we endure it patiently.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 4:13"/>
    When we are slandered, we respond with gentle encouragement. We have become like the scum and refuse of the world, the garbage that everyone despises, right up to this present moment.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Paul’s Admonition</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 4:14"/>
    I am not writing these things to shame you, but rather to admonish you as my beloved children.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 4:15"/>
    For even if you have ten thousand instructors or tutors in Christ, you do not have many spiritual fathers, because through the gospel I became your father in Christ Jesus.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 4:16"/>
    Therefore, I urge you earnestly: Become imitators of me and follow my example.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 4:17"/>
    For this very reason, I have sent Timothy to you, who is my beloved and faithful child in the Lord. He will remind you of my ways and manner of life in Christ Jesus, which are the same as I teach in every place and in every church.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 4:18"/>
    But some among you have become arrogant, assuming that I am not coming to visit you.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 4:19"/>
    But I will come to you soon, if the Lord permits, and then I will discover not merely the talk and boasting of those who have become arrogant, but what actual power they possess.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 4:20"/>
    For the kingdom of God does not consist in eloquent talk and empty words, but in the demonstration of power.
    <verse eid="1CO 4:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 4:21"/>
    What is your preference? Shall I come to you with stern discipline like a rod, or shall I come in love and with a spirit of gentleness?
    <verse eid="1CO 4:21"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 4"/>
  <chapter number="5" style="c" sid="1CO 5"/>
  <para style="s1">On Incest and Other Vices in the Church</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 5:1"/>
    It is widely reported that sexual immorality exists among you, and it is immorality of such a kind that does not even occur among pagan Gentiles—namely, that someone is living in a sexual relationship with his father’s wife.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 5:2"/>
    Yet you have become puffed up with arrogance instead of mourning in sorrow, so that the one who has committed this act might be removed from your fellowship.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 5:3"/>
    For I, though physically absent but present with you in spirit, have already passed judgment on the one who has perpetrated this deed, as if I were physically present with you.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 5:4"/>
    When you are assembled together in the name of our Lord Jesus, and my spirit is with you along with the power of our Lord Jesus,
    <verse eid="1CO 5:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 5:5"/>
    you are to hand this person over to Satan for the destruction of his flesh, so that his spirit may be saved on the day of the Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 5:6"/>
    Your boasting is not appropriate. Do you not realize that a small amount of leaven works its way through the entire batch of dough?
    <verse eid="1CO 5:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 5:7"/>
    Clean out the old leaven so that you may be a fresh batch of dough, just as you are unleavened. For Christ our Passover lamb has been sacrificed.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 5:8"/>
    Therefore, let us celebrate the feast, not with old leaven, nor with the leaven of malice and wickedness, but with the unleavened bread of purity and truth.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 5:9"/>
    I wrote to you in my previous letter not to associate with sexually immoral people—
    <verse eid="1CO 5:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 5:10"/>
    not meaning at all the sexually immoral people of this world, or the greedy and swindlers, or idolaters, since then you would need to leave the world entirely.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 5:11"/>
    But now I am writing to you that you must not associate with anyone who claims to be a believer if he is sexually immoral or greedy or an idolater or a slanderer or a drunkard or a swindler. Do not even eat with such a person.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 5:12"/>
    For what business is it of mine to judge those outside the church? Are you not responsible for judging those inside?
    <verse eid="1CO 5:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 5:13"/>
    But those outside God will judge. Remove the wicked person from among yourselves.
    <verse eid="1CO 5:13"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 5"/>
  <chapter number="6" style="c" sid="1CO 6"/>
  <para style="s1">On Lawsuits Among Believers</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 6:1"/>
    When one of you has a grievance against a fellow believer, how dare you seek legal judgment before unrighteous magistrates instead of before the saints?
    <verse eid="1CO 6:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 6:2"/>
    Are you unaware that the saints will participate in judging the world? If you are destined to judge the entire world, are you truly incompetent to adjudicate trivial disputes?
    <verse eid="1CO 6:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 6:3"/>
    Are you unaware that we will evaluate angels? How much more should we be capable of resolving everyday matters?
    <verse eid="1CO 6:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 6:4"/>
    So if you have disputes regarding everyday affairs, why do you place those whom the church considers least significant in positions of judgment?
    <verse eid="1CO 6:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 6:5"/>
    I mention this to bring shame upon you. Can it really be that not even one wise man exists among you who could arbitrate between his brothers?
    <verse eid="1CO 6:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 6:6"/>
    Instead, one brother initiates legal proceedings against another brother—and does so before those who reject the faith!
    <verse eid="1CO 6:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 6:7"/>
    The mere existence of lawsuits among you already represents total failure. Why not instead accept being wronged? Why not instead accept being cheated?
    <verse eid="1CO 6:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 6:8"/>
    Yet you yourselves commit wrongs and and defraud—and you do these things to your brothers!
    <verse eid="1CO 6:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 6:9"/>
    Are you unaware that those who practice unrighteousness will have no inheritance in God’s kingdom? Do not deceive yourselves: neither those who engage in sexual immorality, nor idol worshipers, nor those who commit adultery, nor men who submit to homosexual acts, nor men who engage in homosexual acts, 
    <verse eid="1CO 6:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 6:10"/>
    nor those who steal, nor the greedy, nor drunkards, nor those who slander others, nor swindlers will inherit God’s kingdom.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 6:11"/>
    Some of you formerly lived in these ways. Yet you have been cleansed, you have been sanctified or set apart as holy, you have been justified or declared righteous through the authority of the Lord Jesus and through the Spirit of our God.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">On Sexual Immorality</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 6:12"/>
    “Everything is permissible for me”—yet not everything proves beneficial. “Everything is permissible for me”—yet I refuse to be dominated by anything.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 6:13"/>
    “Food exists for the stomach’s benefit and the stomach for food’s benefit”—yet God will abolish both. However, the body does not exist for sexual immorality’s sake, but for the Lord’s purposes, and the Lord exists for the body’s sake.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 6:14"/>
    God raised the Lord from death and will likewise raise us through His power.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 6:15"/>
    Are you unaware that your physical bodies constitute parts of Christ? Should I then remove parts of Christ and unite them with a prostitute? Absolutely not!
    <verse eid="1CO 6:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 6:16"/>
    Or are you unaware that whoever unites himself with a prostitute forms one body with her? Scripture declares, “The two shall become one flesh.”
    <verse eid="1CO 6:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 6:17"/>
    However, whoever unites himself with the Lord forms one spirit with Him.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 6:18"/>
    Run away from sexual immorality. Every other sin a man commits occurs outside his body, but whoever engages in sexual immorality sins directly against his own body.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 6:19"/>
    Or are you unaware that your body functions as a sanctuary of the Holy Spirit dwelling within you, whom you received from God, and that you do not possess ownership of yourselves?
    <verse eid="1CO 6:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 6:20"/>
    You were purchased at tremendous cost. Therefore, bring glory to God through your body.
    <verse eid="1CO 6:20"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 6"/>
  <chapter number="7" style="c" sid="1CO 7"/>
  <para style="s1">On Christian Marriage</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 7:1"/>
    Now addressing the matters you wrote about: “Avoiding sexual contact with a woman benefits a man.”
    <verse eid="1CO 7:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 7:2"/>
    However, because sexual immorality occurs so frequently, each man should maintain his own wife, and each woman should maintain her own husband.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 7:3"/>
    The husband should fulfill his marital obligations toward his wife, and likewise the wife should fulfill hers toward her husband.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 7:4"/>
    The wife does not possess independent authority over her own body; rather, her husband does. Likewise, the husband does not possess independent authority over his own body; rather, his wife does.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 7:5"/>
    Do not withhold yourselves from one another, except perhaps by mutual agreement for a limited period, enabling you to dedicate yourselves to prayer. Then resume normal relations, preventing Satan from exploiting your lack of self-restraint to tempt you.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 7:6"/>
    I offer this as a permitted concession, not as a binding directive.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 7:7"/>
    I wish all men were even as I am. However, each man has his own gift from God, one in this manner, another in that manner.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 7:8"/>
    To those who are unmarried and to widows I say: Remaining as I am would benefit them.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 7:9"/>
    However, if they cannot maintain self-control, they should marry. Marriage is preferable to burning with unfulfilled desire.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 7:10"/>
    To those who are married I give this directive (originating not from me but from the Lord): A wife must not separate from her husband.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 7:11"/>
    But if she does separate, she must either remain unmarried or be reconciled to her husband. Similarly, a husband must not divorce his wife.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 7:12"/>
    To the rest I say (speaking from my own authority, not the Lord’s direct command): If any brother has an unbelieving wife who consents to live with him, he must not divorce her.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 7:13"/>
    If any woman has an unbelieving husband who consents to live with her, she must not divorce him.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 7:14"/>
    The unbelieving husband is set apart through his believing wife, and the unbelieving wife is set apart through her believing husband; otherwise your children would be unclean, but now they are regarded as holy because of the believer’s faith.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 7:15"/>
    However, if the unbelieving partner chooses to depart, allow the separation. The brother or the sister is not bound to the marriage in such circumstances, for God has called us to peaceful living.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 7:16"/>
    How can you know, wife, whether you will bring salvation to your husband? How can you know, husband, whether you will save your wife?
    <verse eid="1CO 7:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 7:17"/>
    Nevertheless, as the Lord has assigned to each man, as God has called each man, so let him conduct his life. This is how I establish practice in all the churches.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 7:18"/>
    Was anyone already circumcised when called? He should not attempt to reverse the circumcision. Was anyone uncircumcised when called? He should not undergo circumcision.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 7:19"/>
    Circumcision means nothing and uncircumcision means nothing; what matters is obeying God’s commandments.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 7:20"/>
    Each man should remain in the condition in which he was called.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 7:21"/>
    Were you a slave when called? Do not let it trouble you. But if you can obtain freedom, seize the opportunity.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 7:22"/>
    The man who was called in the Lord while a slave is the Lord’s freedman. Likewise, the man who was called while free is Christ’s slave.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 7:23"/>
    You were purchased at a price; do not become slaves of men.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 7:24"/>
    Each man should remain with God in the condition in which he was called, brothers.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 7:25"/>
    Now concerning virgins: I have no commandment from the Lord, but I offer my judgment as one who has received mercy from the Lord to be trustworthy.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 7:26"/>
    Therefore, I consider this to be beneficial because of the present distress or crisis. It is good for a man to remain in his current condition.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 7:27"/>
    Are you bound to a wife? Do not seek release. Are you unattached to a woman? Do not seek a wife.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="1CO 7:28"/>
    But even if you do marry, you have not sinned; and if a virgin marries, she has not sinned. Nevertheless, such people will experience trouble in this earthly life, and I want to spare you that.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="1CO 7:29"/>
    But this I say, brothers: The time has been shortened, so that from now on even those who have wives should live as though they had none;
    <verse eid="1CO 7:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="1CO 7:30"/>
    and those who mourn, as though they were not mourning; and those who rejoice, as though they were not rejoicing; and those who make purchases, as though they did not possess anything;
    <verse eid="1CO 7:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="1CO 7:31"/>
    and those who utilize the world, as though not making full use of it. For the present form of this world is passing away.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="1CO 7:32"/>
    I want you to be free from anxieties. The unmarried man is concerned about the Lord’s affairs, how he may please the Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="1CO 7:33"/>
    But the married man is concerned about worldly affairs, how he may please his wife,
    <verse eid="1CO 7:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="1CO 7:34"/>
    and his interests are divided. The unmarried woman or the virgin is concerned about the Lord’s affairs, so that she may be holy in both body and spirit. But the married woman is concerned about worldly affairs, how she may please her husband.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="1CO 7:35"/>
    I say this for your own benefit, not to place restrictions on you, but to promote what is appropriate and to secure undistracted devotion to the Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="1CO 7:36"/>
    But if anyone believes he is treating his betrothed improperly, if she is past the bloom of youth and it seems necessary, let him do what he wishes. He commits no sin; let them marry.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="1CO 7:37"/>
    But the man who stands firm in his heart, being under no compulsion and exercising control over his own will, and has decided not to marry his betrothed, will do well.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="1CO 7:38"/>
    Therefore, the man who marries his betrothed does well, and the man who does not marry her will do even better.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="1CO 7:39"/>
    A wife is bound to her husband as long as he lives. But if her husband dies, she is free to marry whomever she wishes, only in the Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="1CO 7:40"/>
    Yet she is more blessed if she remains as she is, in my judgment—and I believe I also have the Spirit of God.
    <verse eid="1CO 7:40"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 7"/>
  <chapter number="8" style="c" sid="1CO 8"/>
  <para style="s1">On Food Sacrificed to Idols</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 8:1"/>
    Now concerning food sacrificed to idols: We know that “we all possess knowledge.” Knowledge inflates with pride, but love builds up.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 8:2"/>
    If anyone imagines he knows something, he does not yet know as he ought to know.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 8:3"/>
    But if anyone loves God, he is known by Him.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 8:4"/>
    Therefore, concerning the consumption of food sacrificed to idols: We know that “an idol is nothing in the world,” and that “there is no God but one.”
    <verse eid="1CO 8:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 8:5"/>
    For even if there are so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth—as indeed there are many “gods” and many “lords”—
    <verse eid="1CO 8:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 8:6"/>
    yet for us there is one God, the Father, from whom all things come and for whom we exist, and there is one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom all things came and through whom we exist.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 8:7"/>
    However, not everyone has this knowledge. Some people, having long been habituated through prior participation in idol worship, still eat the food as something offered to idols, and their conscience, being weak, becomes defiled.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 8:8"/>
    But food does not commend us to God. We are neither disadvantaged if we abstain, nor advantaged if we eat.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 8:9"/>
    But be careful that this right of yours does not somehow become a stumbling block to those who are weak.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 8:10"/>
    For if someone sees you who have knowledge dining in an idol’s temple, will not his conscience, being weak, be emboldened to eat food sacrificed to idols?
    <verse eid="1CO 8:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 8:11"/>
    So the weak man is destroyed by your knowledge—the brother for whom Christ died.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 8:12"/>
    Thus, by sinning against your brothers and wounding their weak conscience, you sin against Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 8:13"/>
    Therefore, if food causes my brother to stumble, I will never eat meat again, so that I may not cause my brother to stumble.
    <verse eid="1CO 8:13"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 8"/>
  <chapter number="9" style="c" sid="1CO 9"/>
  <para style="s1">Paul Gives Up Certain of His Rights</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 9:1"/>
    Am I not operating as a free person? Do I not function as an apostle? Have I not personally witnessed Jesus our Lord? Are you not the evidence of my apostolic work in the Lord?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 9:2"/>
    Even if others do not recognize my apostolic authority, surely you must, for you serve as the validation of my apostleship in the Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 9:3"/>
    This is my response to those who question and scrutinize me:
    <verse eid="1CO 9:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 9:4"/>
    Do we not have the right to receive food and drink?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 9:5"/>
    Do we not have the right to be accompanied by a believing wife, just as the other apostles do, along with the Lord’s brothers and Cephas?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 9:6"/>
    Or are Barnabas and I the only ones expected to continue supporting ourselves through manual labor?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 9:7"/>
    What soldier ever serves in the military at his own financial expense? What farmer plants a vineyard but does not consume its fruit? What shepherd tends a flock but does not drink from the flock’s milk?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 9:8"/>
    I am not presenting these arguments from a merely human perspective. Does not the Law itself affirm these principles?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 9:9"/>
    For in the Law of Moses it is written: “You shall not muzzle an ox while it is treading out grain.”* Is God primarily concerned about oxen?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 9:10"/>
    Or is He undoubtedly speaking with us in mind? Indeed, this was written for our instruction, because the one who plows should plow with expectation, and the one who threshes grain should do so with hope of sharing in the harvest.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 9:11"/>
    If we have planted spiritual realities in your lives, is it excessive for us to harvest material support from you?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 9:12"/>
    If others exercise this right to receive support from you, do we not have an even greater claim? Nevertheless, we have not exercised this right, but instead endure all hardships patiently, to avoid placing any obstacle in the path of Christ’s gospel.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 9:13"/>
    Do you not understand that those performing temple duties receive their sustenance from the temple, and those serving at the altar share in the altar’s offerings?
    <verse eid="1CO 9:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 9:14"/>
    In the same way, the Lord has ordained that those who proclaim the gospel should derive their living from gospel ministry.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 9:15"/>
    But I have not utilized any of these privileges. And I am not writing these things to secure such treatment for myself. It would be preferable for me to die rather than allow anyone to nullify my grounds for boasting.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 9:16"/>
    For if I proclaim the gospel, this gives me no basis for boasting, because compulsion has been placed upon me. Indeed, disaster would befall me if I failed to proclaim the gospel.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 9:17"/>
    For if I do this willingly, I receive a reward; but if unwillingly, I have simply been entrusted with a stewardship responsibility.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 9:18"/>
    What then is my reward? That in preaching the gospel, I may offer the gospel free of charge, so as not to exploit fully my rights in gospel ministry.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Paul’s Enslavement for Ministry’s Purpose</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 9:19"/>
    For though I am free from obligation to anyone, I have made myself a servant to everyone, in order to gain as many as possible.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 9:20"/>
    I have become like a Jewish person to Jewish people, in order to gain Jewish people. To those living under the Law, I became as one under the Law (though I myself am not under the Law’s jurisdiction), in order to gain those under the Law.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 9:21"/>
    To those without the Law, I became as one without the law (though I am not lawless before God, but under Christ’s law), in order to gain those without the Law.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 9:22"/>
    I became weak to the weak, in order to gain the weak. I have become all things to all people, so that by all possible means I might save some.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 9:23"/>
    I do all these things for the gospel’s sake, so that I may become a fellow participant in its benefits.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Paul’s Bodily Discipline</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 9:24"/>
    Do you not know that all runners in a stadium compete, but only one receives the prize? Run in such a way that you may obtain it.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 9:25"/>
    Everyone who competes in athletic contests exercises strict self-discipline in every area. They do this to receive a perishable crown, but we compete for an imperishable one.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 9:26"/>
    Therefore, I run with clear purpose and direction, not aimlessly. I box with precision, not as one merely striking the air.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 9:27"/>
    I subject my body to rigorous discipline and bring it under strict control, lest after preaching to others, I myself should be rejected as unqualified.
    <verse eid="1CO 9:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">9 Quoted from Deut. 25:4</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 9"/>
  <chapter number="10" style="c" sid="1CO 10"/>
  <para style="s1">Examples of Divine Discipline From Israel’s History</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 10:1"/>
    For I do not want you to remain unaware, brothers and sisters, that our ancestors all experienced the cloud’s protection, and all passed through the sea,
    <verse eid="1CO 10:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 10:2"/>
    and all were baptized into identification with Moses in the cloud and in the sea,
    <verse eid="1CO 10:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 10:3"/>
    and all consumed the same supernatural food,
    <verse eid="1CO 10:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 10:4"/>
    and all drank the same supernatural drink. For they were drinking from the supernatural Rock that accompanied them, and that Rock was Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 10:5"/>
    Yet God was not pleased with most of them, for their bodies were scattered across the wilderness.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 10:6"/>
    Now these events occurred as examples for our instruction, so that we would not crave evil things as they craved them.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 10:7"/>
    Do not become idolaters, as some of them did, just as Scripture records: “The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to engage in revelry.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 10:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 10:8"/>
    Nor should we engage in sexual immorality, as some of them did, resulting in twenty-three thousand falling under judgment in a single day.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 10:9"/>
    Nor should we test Christ, as some of them tested Him and were destroyed by serpents.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 10:10"/>
    Nor should we complain, as some of them complained and were destroyed by the destroying angel.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 10:11"/>
    Now these events happened to them as warnings, and they were recorded for our instruction, upon whom the culmination of the ages has come.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 10:12"/>
    Therefore, let the person who thinks he stands firm take care lest he fall.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 10:13"/>
    No temptation has seized you except what is common to humanity. But God is faithful, who will not permit you to be tempted beyond your capacity, but with the temptation will also provide the way of escape, enabling you to endure it.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Warnings Against Idolatry</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 10:14"/>
    Therefore, my beloved ones, flee from idolatry.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 10:15"/>
    I speak to you as to thoughtful people; judge for yourselves what I am saying.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 10:16"/>
    The cup of blessing that we bless, is it not a participation in Christ’s blood? The bread that we break, is it not a participation in Christ’s body?
    <verse eid="1CO 10:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 10:17"/>
    Because there is one loaf, we who are many constitute one body, for we all partake of the one loaf.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 10:18"/>
    Consider Israel according to physical descent: Are not those who eat the sacrifices participants in the altar?
    <verse eid="1CO 10:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 10:19"/>
    What am I implying then? That food sacrificed to an idol has any significance, or that an idol is anything?
    <verse eid="1CO 10:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 10:20"/>
    No, but I am saying that what pagans sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons and not to God; and I do not want you to become participants with demons.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 10:21"/>
    You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and also the cup of demons. You cannot partake of the Lord’s table and also the table of demons.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 10:22"/>
    Or are we provoking the Lord to jealousy? We are not stronger than He is, are we?
    <verse eid="1CO 10:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Extent of Christian Freedom</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 10:23"/>
    “Everything is permissible,” but not everything is beneficial. “Everything is permissible,” but not everything builds up the community.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 10:24"/>
    Let no one pursue their own advantage, but rather let each one pursue the advantage of others.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 10:25"/>
    Eat whatever is sold in the meat market, raising no questions on the basis of conscience.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 10:26"/>
    For “The earth belongs to the Lord, and everything in it.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 10:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 10:27"/>
    If an unbeliever invites you to a meal and you wish to go, eat whatever is set before you, raising no questions on the basis of conscience.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="1CO 10:28"/>
    But if someone says to you, “This has been offered in sacrifice to an idol,” do not eat it, for the sake of the one who informed you and for the sake of conscience.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="1CO 10:29"/>
    Now when I say “conscience,” I mean not your own conscience, but the conscience of the other person. For why should my freedom be subject to judgment by another person’s conscience?
    <verse eid="1CO 10:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="1CO 10:30"/>
    If I partake with thankfulness, why am I defamed concerning something for which I give thanks?
    <verse eid="1CO 10:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="1CO 10:31"/>
    Therefore, whether you eat or drink, or whatever you do, do everything to bring glory to God.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="1CO 10:32"/>
    Give no offense, whether to Jewish people or to Greek people, or to the church of God,
    <verse eid="1CO 10:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="1CO 10:33"/>
    just as I also please everyone in every way, not seeking my own benefit, but the benefit of many, so that they may be saved.
    <verse eid="1CO 10:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">7 Quoted from Ex. 32:6</para>
  <para style="p">26 Quoted from Ps. 24:1</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 10"/>
  <chapter number="11" style="c" sid="1CO 11"/>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 11:1"/>
    Become imitators of me, just as I myself am an imitator of Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">On Head Coverings in Public Worship</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 11:2"/>
    Now I commend you because you remember me in all matters, and you hold fast to the traditions exactly as I transmitted them to you.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 11:3"/>
    But I want you to understand that Christ is the head of every man, and the man is the head of the woman, and God is the head of Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 11:4"/>
    Every man who prays or prophesies while having something covering his head dishonors his head.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 11:5"/>
    And every woman who prays or prophesies with her head uncovered dishonors her head, for she is exactly the same as a woman whose head has been shaved.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 11:6"/>
    For if a woman does not cover her head, let her hair be cut off as well. But if it is disgraceful for a woman to have her hair cut off or her head shaved, let her cover her head.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 11:7"/>
    For a man ought not to cover his head, since he is the image and glory of God, but the woman is the glory of the man.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 11:8"/>
    For man did not come from woman, but woman came from man.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 11:9"/>
    For indeed man was not created for the woman’s sake, but woman was created for the man’s sake.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 11:10"/>
    As a result, the woman ought to have a symbol of authority on her head, because of the angels.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 11:11"/>
    However, in the Lord, woman does not exist in isolation from man, nor does man exist in isolation from woman.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 11:12"/>
    For just as woman came from man, so also man comes through woman. But all things originate from God.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 11:13"/>
    Judge for yourselves: Is it proper for a woman to pray to God with her head uncovered?
    <verse eid="1CO 11:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 11:14"/>
    Does not even nature itself teach you that if a man has long hair, it is a dishonor to him,
    <verse eid="1CO 11:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 11:15"/>
    but if a woman has long hair, it is a glory to her? For long hair has been given to her as a covering.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 11:16"/>
    But if anyone is disposed  to be contentious, we have no such practice, nor do the churches of God.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Divisions and the Lord’s Supper</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 11:17"/>
    Now, in giving these instructions, I do not commend you, because you gather together not for the better, but for the worse.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 11:18"/>
    For in the first place, when you gather together as a church, I hear that divisions exist among you, and I partly believe it.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 11:19"/>
    For factions must indeed exist among you, so that those who are approved may become evident among you.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 11:20"/>
    Therefore, when you assemble in the same place, it is not to eat the Lord’s Supper.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 11:21"/>
    For in eating, each one takes his own meal first; and one person remains hungry, while another becomes drunk.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 11:22"/>
    What! Do you not have homes in which to eat and drink? Or do you show contempt for the church of God and humiliate those who have nothing? What should I say to you? Should I commend you? In this matter, I do not commend you.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 11:23"/>
    For I received from the Lord what I also transmitted to you, that the Lord Jesus, on the night when He was betrayed, took bread,
    <verse eid="1CO 11:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 11:24"/>
    and after giving thanks, He broke it and said, “This is My body, which is for you. Do this in remembrance of Me.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 11:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 11:25"/>
    In the same manner, He also took the cup after the meal, saying, “This cup is the new covenant in My blood. Do this, as often as you drink it, in remembrance of Me.”
    <verse eid="1CO 11:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 11:26"/>
    For as often as you eat this bread and drink this cup, you are proclaiming the Lord’s death until He comes.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 11:27"/>
    Therefore, whoever eats the bread or drinks the cup of the Lord in an unworthy manner will be guilty of sinning against the body and blood of the Lord.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="1CO 11:28"/>
    But let a person examine himself, and in this manner let them eat from the bread and drink from the cup.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="1CO 11:29"/>
    For the one who eats and drinks, eats and drinks judgment upon himself if he does not properly discern the body.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="1CO 11:30"/>
    As a result, many among you are weak and sick, and a considerable number have died.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="1CO 11:31"/>
    But if we were judging ourselves rightly, we would not come under judgment.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="1CO 11:32"/>
    But when we are judged by the Lord, we are being disciplined, so that we will not be condemned along with the world.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="1CO 11:33"/>
    Therefore, my brothers and sisters, when you assemble to eat, wait for one another.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="1CO 11:34"/>
    If anyone is hungry, let him eat at home, so that you do not gather together for judgment. And I will arrange the remaining matters when I come.
    <verse eid="1CO 11:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">24 See Lk. 22:19</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 11"/>
  <chapter number="12" style="c" sid="1CO 12"/>
  <para style="s1">On the Spiritual Gifts</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 12:1"/>
    Now concerning spiritual gifts, brothers and sisters, I do not want you to remain uninformed.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 12:2"/>
    You know that when you were pagans, you were led astray to voiceless idols, however you were influenced.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 12:3"/>
    Therefore, I make known to you that no one speaking by God’s Spirit says, “Jesus is cursed,” and no one can say “Jesus is Lord,” except by the Holy Spirit.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 12:4"/>
    Now there are different kinds of gifts, but the same Spirit distributes them.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 12:5"/>
    And there are different kinds of service, and the same Lord oversees them.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 12:6"/>
    And there are different kinds of working, but the same God produces all of them in everyone.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 12:7"/>
    Now to each individual is given the manifestation of the Spirit for the common good.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 12:8"/>
    For to one person is given through the Spirit the word of wisdom, and to another the word of knowledge according to the same Spirit;
    <verse eid="1CO 12:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 12:9"/>
    to another faith by the same Spirit, and to another gifts of healing by the one Spirit;
    <verse eid="1CO 12:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 12:10"/>
    and to another the working of miracles, and to another prophecy, and to another the ability to distinguish between spirits, to another different kinds of tongues, and to another the interpretation of tongues.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 12:11"/>
    But the one and the same Spirit produces all these things, distributing to each individual just as He determines.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 12:12"/>
    For just as the body is one unit and has many parts, and all the parts of the body, though many, form one body, so also is Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 12:13"/>
    For by one Spirit we were all baptized into one body, whether Jewish or Greek, whether enslaved or free, and all were given one Spirit to drink.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 12:14"/>
    For the body does not consist of one part, but of many parts.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 12:15"/>
    If the foot says, “Because I am not a hand, I do not belong to the body,” it is not for this reason any less part of the body.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 12:16"/>
    And if the ear says, “Because I am not an eye, I do not belong to the body,” it is not for this reason any less part of the body.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 12:17"/>
    If the whole body were an eye, where would the sense of hearing be? If the whole were hearing, where would the sense of smell be?
    <verse eid="1CO 12:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 12:18"/>
    But now God has arranged the parts, each one of them in the body, just as He desired.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 12:19"/>
    And if all were one part, where would the body be?
    <verse eid="1CO 12:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 12:20"/>
    But now there are many parts, yet one body.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 12:21"/>
    The eye cannot say to the hand, “I have no need of you.” Or again, the head cannot say to the feet, “I have no need of you.”
    <verse eid="1CO 12:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 12:22"/>
    On the contrary, the parts of the body that seem to be weaker are indispensable;
    <verse eid="1CO 12:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 12:23"/>
    and the parts of the body that we consider less honorable, these we treat with greater honor, and our less presentable parts receive greater modesty,
    <verse eid="1CO 12:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 12:24"/>
    whereas our presentable parts have no need of this. Yet God has composed the body by giving greater honor to the part that lacked it,
    <verse eid="1CO 12:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 12:25"/>
    so that there may be no division in the body, but that the parts may have the same concern for one another.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 12:26"/>
    And if one part suffers, all the parts suffer with it. If one part is honored, all the parts rejoice with it.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 12:27"/>
    Now you collectively are the body of Christ, and individually you are parts of it.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="1CO 12:28"/>
    And God has appointed in the church first apostles, second prophets, third teachers, then workers of miracles, then gifts of healing, those who help, those who administer, various kinds of tongues.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="1CO 12:29"/>
    Not all are apostles, are they? Not all are prophets, are they? Not all are teachers, are they? Not all work miracles, do they?
    <verse eid="1CO 12:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="1CO 12:30"/>
    Not all have gifts of healing, do they? Not all speak in tongues, do they? Not all interpret, do they?
    <verse eid="1CO 12:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="1CO 12:31"/>
    Now eagerly desire the greater gifts. And I will show you a supremely excellent way.
    <verse eid="1CO 12:31"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 12"/>
  <chapter number="13" style="c" sid="1CO 13"/>
  <para style="s1">A More Excellent Way</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 13:1"/>
    If I speak in the tongues of humans and of angels, but do not have love, I have become a sounding brass or a clanging cymbal.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 13:2"/>
    And if I have the gift of prophecy and understand all mysteries and all knowledge, and if I have complete faith so as to move mountains, yet do not have love, I am nothing.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 13:3"/>
    And if I give away all my possessions to feed the poor, and if I surrender my body so that I may boast, but do not have love, it profits me nothing.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 13:4"/>
    Love is patient, love is kind, love does not envy; it does not boast, it is not puffed up with arrogance,
    <verse eid="1CO 13:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 13:5"/>
    it does not behave improperly; it does not seek its own interests, it is not provoked, it does not keep a record of wrongs;
    <verse eid="1CO 13:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 13:6"/>
    it does not rejoice over unrighteousness, but rejoices with the truth;
    <verse eid="1CO 13:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 13:7"/>
    it bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 13:8"/>
    Love never fails. But if there are prophecies, they will be brought to an end. If there are tongues, they will cease. If there is knowledge, it will be brought to an end.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 13:9"/>
    For we know in part, and we prophesy in part,
    <verse eid="1CO 13:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 13:10"/>
    but when the complete comes, the partial will be brought to an end.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 13:11"/>
    When I was a child, I spoke like a child, I thought like a child, I reasoned like a child. When I became an adult, I put away childish things.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 13:12"/>
    For now we see through a mirror obscurely, but then face to face. Now I know in part, but then I will know fully, just as I also have been fully known.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 13:13"/>
    And now these three remain: faith, hope, love. But the greatest of these is love.
    <verse eid="1CO 13:13"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 13"/>
  <chapter number="14" style="c" sid="1CO 14"/>
  <para style="s1">Prophecy and Tongues</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 14:1"/>
    Pursue love, and eagerly desire spiritual gifts, but especially that you may prophesy.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 14:2"/>
    For the one who speaks in a tongue does not speak to people but to God; for no one understands, but he speaks mysteries by the Spirit.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 14:3"/>
    But the one who prophesies speaks to people for edification and encouragement and consolation.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 14:4"/>
    The one who speaks in a tongue edifies himself, whereas the one who prophesies edifies the church.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 14:5"/>
    Now I want all of you to speak in tongues, but even more that you would prophesy. And the one who prophesies is greater than the one who speaks in tongues, unless he interprets, so that the church may receive edification.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 14:6"/>
    But now, brothers and sisters, if I come to you speaking in tongues, how will I benefit you, unless I speak to you either by revelation, or by knowledge, or by prophecy, or by teaching?
    <verse eid="1CO 14:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 14:7"/>
    Even lifeless instruments that produce sound, whether flute or harp, if they do not produce distinct notes, how will anyone know what is being played on the flute or what is being played on the harp?
    <verse eid="1CO 14:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 14:8"/>
    For indeed, if the trumpet produces an unclear sound, who will prepare themselves for battle?
    <verse eid="1CO 14:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 14:9"/>
    So you also, unless you produce intelligible speech through your tongue, how will anyone understand what is being said? For you will be speaking into empty air.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 14:10"/>
    There are, perhaps, so many kinds of languages in the world, yet none lacks meaning.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 14:11"/>
    If then I do not understand the meaning of the language, I will be a foreigner to the one speaking, and the one speaking will be a foreigner to me.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 14:12"/>
    So also you, since you are zealous for spiritual manifestations, seek to excel in them for the edification of the church. 
    <verse eid="1CO 14:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 14:13"/>
    Therefore, let the one who speaks in a tongue pray that he may interpret.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 14:14"/>
    For if I pray in a tongue, my spirit prays but my mind remains unfruitful.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 14:15"/>
    What is the conclusion then? I will pray with the spirit, and I will also pray with the mind. I will sing praise with the spirit, and I will also sing praise with the mind.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 14:16"/>
    Otherwise, if you offer blessing with the spirit, how will the one who occupies the position of the uninstructed say the “Amen” at your thanksgiving, since he does not know what you are saying?
    <verse eid="1CO 14:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 14:17"/>
    For you are giving thanks well indeed, but the other person is not being edified.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 14:18"/>
    I give thanks to God that I speak in tongues more than all of you, 
    <verse eid="1CO 14:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 14:19"/>
    yet, in the church I would rather speak five words with my mind in order to instruct others as well, rather than ten thousand words in a tongue.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 14:20"/>
    Brothers and sisters, do not be children in your thinking, but in regard to evil, be infants. In your thinking, however, be mature adults.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 14:21"/>
    In the Law, it is written: 
    <verse eid="1CO 14:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">“By people speaking other tongues </para>
  <para style="p">and by lips of foreigners I will speak to this people, </para>
  <para style="p">and even then they will not listen to Me,</para>
  <para style="p">says the Lord.”*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 14:22"/>
    Therefore, tongues serve as a sign, not for believers but for unbelievers, whereas prophecy serves not for unbelievers, but for believers.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 14:23"/>
    If, therefore, the whole church assembles in the same place and everyone speaks in tongues, and uninstructed people or unbelievers enter, will they not say that you are out of your minds?
    <verse eid="1CO 14:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 14:24"/>
    But if all prophesy, and some unbeliever or an uninstructed person enters, he is convicted by all; he is examined by all.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 14:25"/>
    The secrets of his heart are revealed, and consequently, falling on his face, he will worship God, declaring that God is truly among you.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Decency and Order in Worship</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 14:26"/>
    What is the conclusion then, brothers and sisters? When you assemble, each one has a psalm, has a teaching, has a revelation, has a tongue, has an interpretation. Let all things be done for edification.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 14:27"/>
    If anyone speaks in a tongue, let there be only two, or at most three, and each in turn, and let one person interpret.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="1CO 14:28"/>
    But if there is no interpreter, let him keep silent in the church, and let him speak to himself and to God.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="1CO 14:29"/>
    Let two or three prophets speak, and let the others evaluate what is said.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="1CO 14:30"/>
    And if a revelation is given to another who is seated, let the first one keep silent.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="1CO 14:31"/>
    For you can all prophesy one by one, so that all may learn and all may be encouraged;
    <verse eid="1CO 14:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="1CO 14:32"/>
    and the spirits of prophets are subject to prophets.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="1CO 14:33"/>
    For He is not a God of disorder, but of peace. 
    <verse eid="1CO 14:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">As in all the churches of the holy ones,</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="1CO 14:34"/>
    let the women remain silent in the churches. For they are not permitted to speak, but must be submissive, as the Law also says.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="1CO 14:35"/>
    But if they want to learn anything, let them ask their own husbands at home; for it is improper for a woman to speak in the church.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="1CO 14:36"/>
    Or did the word of God originate with you, or did it come to you exclusively?
    <verse eid="1CO 14:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="1CO 14:37"/>
    If anyone considers himself a prophet or spiritual person, let him acknowledge that what I am writing to you is the Lord’s command.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="1CO 14:38"/>
    But if anyone is ignorant, let him remain ignorant.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="1CO 14:39"/>
    Therefore, my brothers and sisters, eagerly desire to prophesy, and do not forbid speaking in tongues.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="1CO 14:40"/>
    But let all things be done properly and in an orderly manner.
    <verse eid="1CO 14:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">21 Quoted from Is. 28:11-12</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 14"/>
  <chapter number="15" style="c" sid="1CO 15"/>
  <para style="s1">The Resurrection of Christ</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 15:1"/>
    Now I make known to you, brothers and sisters, the gospel that I proclaimed to you, which you also received, in which you also stand,
    <verse eid="1CO 15:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 15:2"/>
    by which you are also being saved, if you hold firmly to the message I proclaimed to you, unless you believed in vain.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 15:3"/>
    For I passed on to you as of foremost importance what I also received, that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures,
    <verse eid="1CO 15:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 15:4"/>
    and that He was buried, and that He was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures,
    <verse eid="1CO 15:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 15:5"/>
    and that He appeared to Cephas, then to the Twelve.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 15:6"/>
    After that, He appeared to more than five hundred brothers and sisters at one time, most of whom remain alive until now, though some have fallen asleep.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 15:7"/>
    After that, He appeared to James, then to all the apostles.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 15:8"/>
    Then last of all, as to one abnormally born, He appeared also to me.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 15:9"/>
    For I am the least of the apostles, not fit to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 15:10"/>
    But by God’s grace, I am what I am, and His grace toward me did not prove fruitless. Rather, I worked harder than all of them, yet not I, but the grace of God that was with me.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 15:11"/>
    Therefore, whether it was I or they, this is what we proclaim, and this is what you believed.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Resurrection of the Dead</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 15:12"/>
    Now if Christ is proclaimed as having been raised from the dead, how can some among you say that there is no resurrection of the dead?
    <verse eid="1CO 15:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 15:13"/>
    But if there is no resurrection of the dead, then neither has Christ been raised.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 15:14"/>
    And if Christ has not been raised, then our proclamation is futile, your faith also is futile.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 15:15"/>
    Moreover, we are found to be false witnesses about God, because we testified concerning God that He raised Christ, whom He did not raise—if indeed the dead are not raised.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 15:16"/>
    For if the dead are not raised, then neither has Christ been raised.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 15:17"/>
    And if Christ has not been raised, your faith is worthless; you are still in your sins.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 15:18"/>
    Then also, those who have fallen asleep in Christ have perished completely.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 15:19"/>
    If we have hoped in Christ only for this life, we are of all people most pitiable.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 15:20"/>
    But now Christ has been raised from the dead, the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 15:21"/>
    For since death came through a human being, the resurrection of the dead also comes through a human being.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 15:22"/>
    For just as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all will be made alive.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 15:23"/>
    But each in their own order: Christ the firstfruits; afterward, those who belong to Christ at His coming,
    <verse eid="1CO 15:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 15:24"/>
    then the end, when He hands over the kingdom to God the Father, when He has abolished all rule and all authority and power.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:24"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="25" style="v" sid="1CO 15:25"/>
    For it is necessary for Him to reign until He has put all His enemies under His feet.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:25"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="26" style="v" sid="1CO 15:26"/>
    The last enemy to be abolished is death.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:26"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="27" style="v" sid="1CO 15:27"/>
    For “He has put all things in subjection under His feet.” But when it says that “All things are put in subjection,” it is clear that the One who subjected all things to Him is excluded.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:27"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="28" style="v" sid="1CO 15:28"/>
    Moreover, when all things are subjected to Him, then the Son Himself will be subjected to the One who subjected all things to Him, so that God may be all in all.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:28"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="29" style="v" sid="1CO 15:29"/>
    Otherwise, what will those who are baptized for the dead accomplish? If the dead are not raised at all, why then are they baptized for them?
    <verse eid="1CO 15:29"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="30" style="v" sid="1CO 15:30"/>
    Why do we also face danger every hour?
    <verse eid="1CO 15:30"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="31" style="v" sid="1CO 15:31"/>
    I solemnly affirm, brothers and sisters, by my boasting in you which I have in Christ Jesus our Lord, I die daily.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:31"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="32" style="v" sid="1CO 15:32"/>
    If in merely human terms I fought with wild beasts in Ephesus, what advantage is it to me? If the dead are not raised, “Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 15:32"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="33" style="v" sid="1CO 15:33"/>
    Do not be deceived: “Bad company corrupts good character.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 15:33"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="34" style="v" sid="1CO 15:34"/>
    Come to your senses righteously and stop sinning, for some have no knowledge of God—I say this to your shame.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:34"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Nature of the Believers’ Resurrection Body</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="35" style="v" sid="1CO 15:35"/>
    Now someone will ask, “How are the dead raised? And with what kind of body do they come?”
    <verse eid="1CO 15:35"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="36" style="v" sid="1CO 15:36"/>
    You fool! What you sow does not come to life unless it dies.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:36"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="37" style="v" sid="1CO 15:37"/>
    And what you sow is not the body that will be, but you sow a bare seed—perhaps of wheat or of some other grain.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:37"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="38" style="v" sid="1CO 15:38"/>
    And God gives it a body according to His design, and to each of the seeds its own body.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:38"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="39" style="v" sid="1CO 15:39"/>
    Not all flesh is the same, but there is one kind of flesh of humans, and another flesh of animals, and another flesh of birds, and another of fish.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:39"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="40" style="v" sid="1CO 15:40"/>
    Moreover, there are heavenly bodies and earthly bodies; but the glory of the heavenly bodies is of one kind, and the glory of the earthly bodies is of another kind.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:40"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="41" style="v" sid="1CO 15:41"/>
    There is one glory of the sun, and another glory of the moon, and yet another glory of the stars; for star differs from star in glory.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:41"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="42" style="v" sid="1CO 15:42"/>
    So also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in perishability, it is raised in imperishability.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:42"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="43" style="v" sid="1CO 15:43"/>
    It is sown in dishonor, it is raised in glory. It is sown in weakness, it is raised in power.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:43"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="44" style="v" sid="1CO 15:44"/>
    It is sown a natural body, it is raised a spiritual body. If there exists a natural body, there exists also a spiritual body.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:44"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="45" style="v" sid="1CO 15:45"/>
    So also it is written: “The first man, Adam, became a living being.”* The last Adam became a life-giving spirit.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:45"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="46" style="v" sid="1CO 15:46"/>
    But the spiritual is not first, but the natural; then the spiritual.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:46"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="47" style="v" sid="1CO 15:47"/>
    The first man was from the earth, composed of dust; the second man is from heaven.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:47"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="48" style="v" sid="1CO 15:48"/>
    As was the one composed of dust, so also are those composed of dust; and as is the heavenly one, so also are those who are heavenly.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:48"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="49" style="v" sid="1CO 15:49"/>
    And just as we have borne the image of the one composed of dust, we will also bear the image of the heavenly one.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:49"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">The Mystery of Resurrection</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="50" style="v" sid="1CO 15:50"/>
    Now I declare this, brothers and sisters, that flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God, nor does the perishable inherit the imperishable.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:50"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="51" style="v" sid="1CO 15:51"/>
    Behold, I tell you a mystery: We will not all fall asleep, but we will all be transformed,
    <verse eid="1CO 15:51"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="52" style="v" sid="1CO 15:52"/>
    in an instant, in the blink of an eye, at the last trumpet. For the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised imperishable, and we will be transformed.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:52"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="53" style="v" sid="1CO 15:53"/>
    For it is necessary for this perishable body to put on imperishability, and for this mortal body to put on immortality.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:53"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="54" style="v" sid="1CO 15:54"/>
    But when this perishable body puts on imperishability, and this mortal body puts on immortality, then will come to pass the saying that is written: “Death has been swallowed up in victory.”*
    <verse eid="1CO 15:54"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="55" style="v" sid="1CO 15:55"/>
    “Where, O death, is your victory? 
    <verse eid="1CO 15:55"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">Where, O death, is your sting?”*</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="56" style="v" sid="1CO 15:56"/>
    Now the sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the Law.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:56"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="57" style="v" sid="1CO 15:57"/>
    But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:57"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="58" style="v" sid="1CO 15:58"/>
    Therefore, my beloved brothers and sisters, be steadfast, unmovable, always excelling in the work of the Lord, knowing that in the Lord your labor is not in vain.
    <verse eid="1CO 15:58"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Notes</para>
  <para style="p">32 Quoted from Is. 22:13</para>
  <para style="p">33 Possibly a quote from the Greek poet called Menander</para>
  <para style="p">45 Quoted from Gen. 2:7</para>
  <para style="p">54 See Is. 25:8</para>
  <para style="p">55 Quoted from Hos. 13:14</para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 15"/>
  <chapter number="16" style="c" sid="1CO 16"/>
  <para style="s1">Collection for the Poor Jerusalem Saints</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="1" style="v" sid="1CO 16:1"/>
    Now concerning the collection for the holy ones: Just as I gave instructions to the churches of Galatia, so you also are to do.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:1"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="2" style="v" sid="1CO 16:2"/>
    On the first day of every week, let each one of you set aside some amount and store it up as he may prosper, so that no collections need be taken when I come.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:2"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="3" style="v" sid="1CO 16:3"/>
    And when I arrive, whomever you approve, I will send them with letters to carry your gift to Jerusalem;
    <verse eid="1CO 16:3"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="4" style="v" sid="1CO 16:4"/>
    and if it is considered appropriate for me to go also, they will travel with me.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:4"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Travel Plans of Paul and Others</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="5" style="v" sid="1CO 16:5"/>
    But I will come to you after I pass through Macedonia, for I am passing through Macedonia,
    <verse eid="1CO 16:5"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="6" style="v" sid="1CO 16:6"/>
    and perhaps I will stay with you, or even spend the winter, so that you may send me on my way wherever I go.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:6"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="7" style="v" sid="1CO 16:7"/>
    For I do not want to see you now just in passing; for I hope to spend some time with you, if the Lord permits.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:7"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="8" style="v" sid="1CO 16:8"/>
    But I will remain in Ephesus until Pentecost,
    <verse eid="1CO 16:8"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="9" style="v" sid="1CO 16:9"/>
    for a wide and effective door has opened to me, but there are many opponents.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:9"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="10" style="v" sid="1CO 16:10"/>
    Now if Timothy comes, see that he is with you without any cause for fear, for he is carrying out the Lord’s work, as I also am.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:10"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="11" style="v" sid="1CO 16:11"/>
    Therefore, let no one treat him with contempt, but send him on his way in peace, so that he may come to me; for I am expecting him along with the brothers and sisters.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:11"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="12" style="v" sid="1CO 16:12"/>
    Now concerning our brother Apollos, I strongly urged him to come to you with the brothers. However, it was not at all his desire to come at this time, but he will come when he has the opportunity.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:12"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Final Encouragement</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="13" style="v" sid="1CO 16:13"/>
    Be alert, stand firm in the faith, act courageously, be strong.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:13"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="14" style="v" sid="1CO 16:14"/>
    Let everything you do be done in love.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:14"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="15" style="v" sid="1CO 16:15"/>
    Now I urge you, brothers and sisters—you know that the household of Stephanas is the firstfruits of Achaia, and they have devoted themselves to the service of the holy ones—
    <verse eid="1CO 16:15"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="16" style="v" sid="1CO 16:16"/>
    that you also submit to such people, and to everyone who cooperates in the work and labors.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:16"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="17" style="v" sid="1CO 16:17"/>
    And I rejoice over the arrival of Stephanas and Fortunatus and Achaicus, because they have supplied what was lacking from you,
    <verse eid="1CO 16:17"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="18" style="v" sid="1CO 16:18"/>
    for they have refreshed my spirit and yours. Therefore, give recognition to such people.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:18"/>
  </para>
  <para style="s1">Final Greetings and Benediction</para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="19" style="v" sid="1CO 16:19"/>
    The churches of Asia send you greetings. Aquila and Prisca greet you warmly in the Lord, along with the church in their house.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:19"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="20" style="v" sid="1CO 16:20"/>
    All the brothers and sisters send you greetings. Greet one another with a holy kiss.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:20"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="21" style="v" sid="1CO 16:21"/>
    The greeting is in my own hand—Paul’s.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:21"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="22" style="v" sid="1CO 16:22"/>
    If anyone does not love the Lord, let him be accursed. O Lord, come!
    <verse eid="1CO 16:22"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="23" style="v" sid="1CO 16:23"/>
    The grace of the Lord Jesus be with you.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:23"/>
  </para>
  <para style="p">
    <verse number="24" style="v" sid="1CO 16:24"/>
    My love be with all of you in Christ Jesus. Amen.
    <verse eid="1CO 16:24"/>
  </para>
  <chapter eid="1CO 16"/>
</usx>
